1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,824
Most creative portfolios are a wall of finished ads, arranged to look impressive.

2
00:00:04,824 --> 00:00:09,132
That's proof of production, not proof of the system this course teaches, and a

3
00:00:09,132 --> 00:00:13,605
prospect who's evaluating whether you can find a winner, not just make one, needs

4
00:00:13,605 --> 00:00:15,152
different evidence entirely.

5
00:00:15,152 --> 00:00:19,515
A case study built from Section 9.5's report format is that different evidence.

6
00:00:19,515 --> 00:00:24,099
It shows the objective, the metric that answered it, the honest ratio across a real

7
00:00:24,099 --> 00:00:27,468
batch, and the one creative that actually won, in that order.

8
00:00:27,468 --> 00:00:29,125
That order is the whole point.

9
00:00:29,125 --> 00:00:33,102
It proves you followed a process to the winner, rather than picking your

10
00:00:33,102 --> 00:00:35,366
best-looking output and calling it proof.

11
00:00:35,366 --> 00:00:39,619
Sam's own case study is exactly this, built from his real reports on Harlan's

12
00:00:39,619 --> 00:00:41,773
account, redacted rather than invented.

13
00:00:41,773 --> 00:00:44,093
Nothing in it needed writing from scratch.

14
00:00:44,093 --> 00:00:48,677
It already existed, one honest weekly report at a time, from the moment Section 9.5

15
00:00:48,677 --> 00:00:50,057
taught him to write them.

16
00:00:50,057 --> 00:00:54,586
Why does this matter enough to teach as its own lecture, rather than a single line

17
00:00:54,586 --> 00:00:56,133
saying "reuse your reports"?

18
00:00:56,133 --> 00:01:00,330
Because the instinct to build a portfolio from scratch, polished and written

19
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:04,914
specifically to impress, is strong, and it's exactly the instinct that produces the

20
00:01:04,914 --> 00:01:08,007
highlight reel this lecture is trying to help you avoid.

21
00:01:08,557 --> 00:01:11,660
Notice the right column is where the actual value lives.

22
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:16,203
A prospect doesn't need to know it was specifically compression socks to trust the

23
00:01:16,203 --> 00:01:16,646
process.

24
00:01:16,646 --> 00:01:21,300
They need to see that a real decision tree was applied, a real ratio resulted, and a

25
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:25,067
real winner survived scrutiny rather than being declared on day one.

26
00:01:25,067 --> 00:01:29,610
Redacting the left column isn't just good practice with a client's data, it's also

27
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:31,937
what makes the case study honest to reuse.

28
00:01:31,937 --> 00:01:35,261
You're not claiming credit for someone else's brand success.

29
00:01:35,261 --> 00:01:39,694
You're demonstrating a repeatable process that happened to run on their account,

30
00:01:39,694 --> 00:01:42,242
which is a narrower and more defensible claim.

31
00:01:42,242 --> 00:01:46,674
Get explicit permission before using any client's numbers at all, even redacted.

32
00:01:46,674 --> 00:01:49,555
Some clients are fine with an anonymised case study.

33
00:01:49,555 --> 00:01:54,098
Some aren't, and assuming consent because the brand name is hidden is not the same

34
00:01:54,098 --> 00:01:55,317
as actually having it.

35
00:01:55,317 --> 00:01:59,971
If a client declines, that's not the end of the case study, only a constraint on how

36
00:01:59,971 --> 00:02:01,024
specific it can be.

37
00:02:01,024 --> 00:02:05,622
A version that rounds every number and describes the category rather than naming it

38
00:02:05,622 --> 00:02:09,833
still shows the same process, the same ratio shape, the same honest verdict.

39
00:02:09,833 --> 00:02:14,209
The identifying detail is the least important part of what makes it convincing.

40
00:02:14,759 --> 00:02:19,168
Five lines, pulled directly from reports you've already written if you've been

41
00:02:19,168 --> 00:02:21,202
running Section 9's sprint honestly.

42
00:02:21,202 --> 00:02:25,893
There's no new document to draft from a blank page here, only a compilation of what

43
00:02:25,893 --> 00:02:27,871
the weekly rhythm already produced.

44
00:02:27,871 --> 00:02:31,771
Line five is the line most competing portfolios can't honestly write.

45
00:02:31,771 --> 00:02:36,236
"This took a process, not luck" only means something if the four lines above it

46
00:02:36,236 --> 00:02:40,983
actually show a process: an objective set in advance, a ratio that includes noise, a

47
00:02:40,983 --> 00:02:44,035
verdict read against a tree rather than a gut feeling.

48
00:02:44,035 --> 00:02:47,709
Claim it without the evidence and it reads as marketing language.

49
00:02:47,709 --> 00:02:50,421
Claim it with the evidence and it's simply true.

50
00:02:50,421 --> 00:02:53,417
Notice, too, that line four is optional for a reason.

51
00:02:53,417 --> 00:02:58,108
Not every sprint cycle you'd want to feature has a fatigue story yet, especially an

52
00:02:58,108 --> 00:02:58,673
early one.

53
00:02:58,673 --> 00:03:03,138
A case study that skips a line it genuinely has nothing to say about is honest.

54
00:03:03,138 --> 00:03:07,433
One that invents a fatigue story to fill the template is exactly the kind of

55
00:03:07,433 --> 00:03:10,598
embellishment this whole course has been built to avoid.

56
00:03:11,148 --> 00:03:15,508
One honest trap worth naming, because a portfolio piece invites it more than a

57
00:03:15,508 --> 00:03:16,570
weekly report does.

58
00:03:16,570 --> 00:03:19,700
A report goes out every week regardless of how it reads.

59
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:24,395
A portfolio piece gets chosen, which means there's a real decision about which cycle

60
00:03:24,395 --> 00:03:28,866
to feature, and the temptation is to feature the best one you've ever had rather

61
00:03:28,866 --> 00:03:30,320
than a representative one.

62
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:34,903
That's a subtler version of the same dishonesty Section 9.5 already named, dressed

63
00:03:34,903 --> 00:03:36,747
up as normal editorial judgement.

64
00:03:36,747 --> 00:03:39,318
The fix is the same one that fixed the report.

65
00:03:39,318 --> 00:03:44,013
Include the honest ratio in the case study itself, not just the winning result, even

66
00:03:44,013 --> 00:03:45,914
in the cycle you're most proud of.

67
00:03:45,914 --> 00:03:50,553
A prospect who sees noise acknowledged inside your best example trusts it more, not

68
00:03:50,553 --> 00:03:52,845
less, than one shown only a spotless win.

69
00:03:52,845 --> 00:03:57,484
Would a spotless case study actually survive a follow-up question about what didn't

70
00:03:57,484 --> 00:03:57,763
work?

71
00:03:57,763 --> 00:04:02,458
It wouldn't, and a prospect sharp enough to ask that question is exactly the kind of

72
00:04:02,458 --> 00:04:03,576
client worth having.

73
00:04:03,576 --> 00:04:08,271
Building the honest ratio in from the start means that question has an answer ready,

74
00:04:08,271 --> 00:04:12,128
rather than an admission that feels like it's walking something back.

75
00:04:12,678 --> 00:04:17,304
Next lecture turns everything from this section into an actual plan for your first

76
00:04:17,304 --> 00:04:21,873
thirty, sixty, and ninety days, including exactly when a case study like this one

77
00:04:21,873 --> 00:04:23,171
becomes worth building.

78
00:04:23,171 --> 00:04:27,176
Before that, look back through whatever reports you've already written.

79
00:04:27,176 --> 00:04:31,915
If even one sprint cycle has a real ratio and a real verdict in it, you already have

80
00:04:31,915 --> 00:04:33,607
the beginning of a case study.

81
00:04:33,607 --> 00:04:35,694
It just needs redacting, not writing.

82
00:04:35,694 --> 00:04:37,669
Keep this habit going forward, too.

83
00:04:37,669 --> 00:04:41,956
Every future sprint cycle is a potential case study the moment it's reported

84
00:04:41,956 --> 00:04:45,172
honestly, not something to reconstruct later from memory.

85
00:04:45,172 --> 00:04:49,628
The best time to write a case study is the same week the report that becomes it

86
00:04:49,628 --> 00:04:50,475
already exists.
